          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The formalism of
            %em Markov categories
            and
            %em copy-discard categories
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+approach+to+probability+theory"} categorical approach to probability theory
            introduced by
            %a{:href => "#cd_categories"} Cho &amp; Jacobs 2019
            and
            %a{:href => "#fritzmarkov"} Fritz 2020
            (and by other authors independently, see the
            %a{:href => "#History"} History
            section).
          %p
            Intuitively, a Markov category is a category where morphisms behave like “random functions”, for example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernels"} Markov kernels
            form such a category (hence the name). Canonical examples are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+categories"} Kleisli categories
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
          %p
            Markov categories have a graphical formalism, based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            %em generalize
            several results of probability and related fields (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+probability#main_results"} Categorical probability – results
            for more details).
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A concise definition, which we interpret below, can be given as follows: A
            %strong Markov category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+category"} semicartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,\otimes,1)
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supply+in+a+monoidal+category"} supplies
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocommutative+comonoids"} cocommutative comonoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+monoid"} internal comonoid
            compatible with the tensor product.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comultiplication"} comultiplication
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit"} counit
            maps are usually denoted by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi copy
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} copy: X \to X \otimes X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi del
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} del: X\to 1
          %p
            Let’s now restate the definition in a more intuitive, mathematically equivalent way, by making use of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
          %h3#string_diagrams_for_monoidal_categories String diagrams for monoidal categories
          %p
            It is useful to introduce a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            notation for Markov categories, which is used throughout the literature, and which makes stochastic interactions particularly intuitive.
          %p
            First of all, we represent a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            as follows, which we read
            = succeed ":" do
              %em from bottom to top
          %svg{:height => "87.997pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 23.073 87.997", :width => "23.073pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
