          %p
            This highlights that this situation is
            = succeed ":" do
              %em non-signalling
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            depends on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            only, and not on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            above,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may depend both on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            Similarly, one can form the product of three or more objects and morphisms. Note that in the diagrams, the product is strictly associative: string diagrams technically represent, rather than the original category, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+monoidal+category"} strict monoidal category
            which is
            %span.newWikiWord
              monoidally equivalent
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/monoidal+equivalence"} ?
            to the original one; such a category always exists thanks to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorem+for+monoidal+categories"} coherence theorem
          %p In a similar vein, the monoidal unit usually does not appear explicitly in string diagrams, providing this elision does not cause confusion. This way, for example, the diagram
