            a commutative comonoid, and that these maps are compatible with the tensor product. Since the monoidal unit is terminal, we have a Markov category.
          %p The same can be done in every cartesian monoidal category:
          %p
          #cartmon1.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p In a cartesian monoidal category every object is equipped with a unique commutative comonoid structure, defined in terms of the universal property of the product.
          %p In cartesian monoidal categories, no randomness is present (as defined below), and so the Markov structure is purely about manipulating information deterministically: information can be copied, discarded, and processed deterministically (through arbitrary morphisms).
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Note that, while the copy-discard structure of
              %em Set
              and of other cartesian monoidal categories may seem trivial, it is not. The copy map is implicitly used whenever we are
              %em reusing
              a variable in an expression. For example, every function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\times X\to Y
              also defines a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f':X\to Y
              by just “plugging in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              twice”:
