            %p
              Similarly, the discard map is used whenever we are
              %em not using
              a variable in an expression. For example, every function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:X\to Y
              also defines a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g':X\times A\to Y
              which “does not really depend on
              = succeed "\u201D:" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g'(x,a) = g(x) .
            %p
