            such that
          #eq:norm.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sum_{y\in Y} f(y|x)  = 1
          %p
            for every
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
          %p
            We can interpret the number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y|x)
            as the probability of transitioning from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+map#stochastic_maps_from_deterministic_functions"} every deterministic function defines a stochastic matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            is an ordinary function, we can write a stochastic matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            by saying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_f(y|x)=1
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y=f(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            otherwise. Therefore these random transitions include the deterministic ones as a special case.
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+map"} stochastic map
            for more information, and for how they compose.
          %p
