          %p
            is the
            %em product probability
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (p\otimes q)(x,y) = p(x)\,q(y) ,
          %p which as usual denotes independent events.
          %p
            We define the copy and discard maps as the maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi copy
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_copy
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi del
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_del
            induced by the ones of
            %em Set
            (see the example above). Explicitly, the stochastic map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi copy
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} copy:X\to X\otimes X
            is defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi copy
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ″
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo ′
                            %mo =
                            %mi x
                            %mo ″
                            %mo =
                            %mi x
                            %mo ;
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi otherwise
                            %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  copy(x',x''|x) = \begin{cases}
                  1 &amp; x'=x''=x ; \\
                  0 &amp; otherwise.
                  \\end{cases}
          %p
            The stochastic map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi del
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} del:X\to 1
            satisfies
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi del
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  del(*|x) = 1 ,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            is the unique element of the terminal set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            Again, one can check that these maps make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a commutative comonoid, and that these maps are compatible with the tensor product.
          %p
            The reason why we have a
            %em Markov
            category and not just a CD category is exactly the normalization condition
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:norm"} (1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi del
                    %mi Y
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi del
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} del_Y\circ f = del_X
            reads exactly as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi del
                    %mi Y
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mi del
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} del
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (del_Y\circ f) (*|x) = \sum_{y\in Y} del_Y(*|y) \, f(y|x) = \sum_{y\in Y} 1\, f(y|x) = 1 = \del_X(*|x)
