            is the probability of transitioning into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            if we are in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel"} Markov kernel
            for more on this.)
          %p
            The category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            of measurable spaces and Markov kernels has a Markov category structure similar to the one of
            = succeed "." do
              %em FinStoch
            = succeed "," do
              %em FinStoch
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mathcal{A})
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,\mathcal{B})
            (or more briefly,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\otimes Y
            to be the cartesian product of sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} sigma-algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}\otimes\mathcal{B}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B\subseteq X\times Y
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in\mathcal{A}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\in\mathcal{B}
            = succeed "," do
              %em FinStoch
          %p
            The copy and discard maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to X\otimes X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to 1
            %em FinStoch
            (see above), are inherited from the ones of
            = succeed "." do
              %em Set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mathcal{A})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            and for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,A'\in\mathcal{A}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi copy
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi A
                            %mo ∩
                            %mi A
                            %mo ′
                            %mo ;
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∉
                            %mi A
                            %mo ∩
                            %mi A
                            %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  copy(A\times A'|x) = \delta(A\cap A'|x) = \begin{cases}
                  1 &amp; x\in A\cap A' ; \\
                  0 &amp; x\notin A\cap A'
                  \\end{cases}
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi del
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  del(\{*\}|x) = 1 ,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            is the unique element of the terminal measurable space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p These again equip each object with a commutative comonoid structure compatible with the tensor product.
          %p
            Just as for
            = succeed "," do
              %em FinStoch
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is terminal in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            reflects the fact that Markov kernels are normalized: for every kernel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            and for every
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi del
                    %mi Y
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mi del
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi dy
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Y
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi dy
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} del
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (del_Y\circ f) (\{*\}|x) = \int_Y del_Y(\{*\}|y) \, f(dy|x) = \int_Y 1\, f(dy|x) = 1 = \del_X(\{*\}|x) .
          %h3#kleisli_categories_of_probability_monads Kleisli categories of probability monads
          %p
            As explained in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monad"} probability monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            can be used to “add extra morphisms” to a category, to model behaviors that were not present in the original category. These new morphisms form the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} in computer science
            to model computational effects. In particular, monads can be used to add
            %em stochastic
            maps to deterministic ones (more at
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monad"} probability monad
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#the_copydiscard_structure_of_set"} above
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has a canonical copy and discard structure.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of a
