          %p
            If moreover we have that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            preserves the monoidal unit (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ≅
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P1\cong 1
            , i.e. the monad is
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+monad"} affine
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is terminal in the Kleisli category as well, and so we have a Markov category.
          %p
            Several Markov categories are obtained in this way, where the randomness is, in some sense, generated by the monad. For example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            is the Kleisli category of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
          %p
            In fact, all Markov categories fully faithfully monoidally embed in Markov categories of this form, see Theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#theorem:embed-in-representable"}
          %h2#core_concepts Core concepts
          %h3#states_channels_joints_marginals States, channels, joints, marginals
          %p The Markov category structures can be interpreted in terms of probability, statistics and information theory in these ways.
          %p
