          %p
            As we have already remarked, these can be thought of as
            = succeed "," do
              %em functions with possibly random outcomes
            = succeed "." do
              %em potentially noisy channels
          %ul
            %li
              Morphisms of
              %em FinStoch
              are
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+maps"} stochastic maps
            %li
              Morphisms of
              %em Stoch
              are
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernels"} Markov kernels
            %li
              Morphisms of
              %em Set
              (no randomness!) are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
          %p
            Morphisms in a Markov category have an additional useful interpretation: a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            can also be seen as a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                state on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                which depends by a parameter
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %em FinStoch
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y|x)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              which depends by a parameter
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            %li
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %em Stoch
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:(X,\mathcal{A})\to (Y,\mathcal{B})
              of values
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(B|x)
              can be seen as a probability measure on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              which depends
              %em measurably
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel#regular_conditional_distributions"} regular conditional distribution
            %li
              In
              %em Set
              (no randomness!), a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
              can be seen as a point of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              parametrized by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This idea can be seen as a stochastic version of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+elements"} generalized elements
          %p
            Let’s now turn to slightly more complex objects. States in the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:I\to X\otimes Y
            are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong joint states
          %svg{:height => "59.613pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 44.961 59.613", :width => "44.961pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
