          %p
            This can be interpreted as the fact that
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                are independent for each choice of fixed input
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %em FinStoch
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is in the form
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      p(x,y|w) = p_X(x|w)\,p_Y(y|w) ,
              %p which recovers the usual conditional independence in the discrete case.
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Stoch
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subseteq X
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\subseteq Y
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      p(A\times B|w) = p_X(A|w)\,p_Y(B|w) ,
              %p which recovers the usual conditional independence in the measure-theoretic case.
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Set
          %p
            We can interpret the string diagram as follows. In the case of conditional independence, it is not true anymore, in general, that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are fully disconnected. However, they are connected
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                only through
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may tell us something about
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                none of that information is new to us if we already know
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            For example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            could be the number of mathematicians in a random city, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            could be annual consumption of coffee in the same city. We might observe a correlation between the two. However, both the amount of mathematicians and the consumption of coffee correlate with the population size of the city,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                given
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "," do
              %em only looking at cities with similar population size
          %ul
            %li If we don’t know the population size, knowing the coffee consumption may help us estimating the number of mathematicians;
            %li
              If we already know the population size, knowing the coffee consumption does not give us a more accurate estimate about the number of mathematicians. One says that the knowledge of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              %em explains away
              the correlation between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          %p (This is just an example, real data about mathematicians and coffee may differ.)
          %p Once again, the inferential information flow is perfectly reflected by the topological properties of the string diagram. This is one of the main assets of Markov categories: no matter how complex the model is, there always a perfect correspondence between the diagram topology and the interaction structure.
          %p
            Let’s now consider the case of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:W\to X\times Y
            is a (deterministic) function, then knowing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            will already tell us everything about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            (and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            can give us, if we know
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#eqdet"}
            below.)
          %p Let’s now make precise the idea of determinism.
          %h3#deterministic_morphisms Deterministic morphisms
          %p
            We have said that Markov categories are about describing morphisms which we can think of as “behaving randomly”. Let’s make this precise, or rather, let’s define precisely what it means to have
            = succeed "." do
              %em no randomness
          %p
            A state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:I\to X
            in a Markov category is called
            %strong deterministic
            if and only if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} copy
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{copy}\circ p = p\otimes p
          %svg{:height => "87.96pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 204.491 87.96", :width => "204.491pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
