                %path{:d => "M -2.222818 12.109779 L 25.86041 12.109779 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.79701;stroke-linecap:butt;stroke-linejoin:miter;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.982431,0,0,-0.982431,94.64861,24.611871)"}
                %path{:d => "M -4.142681 4.782053 C -3.387221 1.911306 -1.701353 0.55943 0.000420228 -0.00120081 C -1.701353 -0.557855 -3.387221 -1.913707 -4.142681 -4.780479 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.79701;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.982431,0,0,-0.982431,120.4449,12.713664)"}
                %path{:d => "M -36.962045 -12.93968 L 24.576128 -12.93968 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.79701;stroke-linecap:butt;stroke-linejoin:miter;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.982431,0,0,-0.982431,94.64861,24.611871)"}
                %path{:d => "M -0.00145023 3.266798 L -0.00145023 -3.265942 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.79701;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:miter;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(-0.982431,0,0,0.982431,58.1392,37.325751)"}
                %path{:d => "M -4.143693 4.780837 C -3.388233 1.914066 -1.698388 0.558214 -0.000591591 0.00155957 C -1.698388 -0.559071 -3.388233 -1.910947 -4.143693 -4.781694 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.79701;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.982431,0,0,-0.982431,119.184175,37.325751)"}
            is a bijection for all objects
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            The map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi samp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} samp
            can be thought of as being the
            %em universal
            random map, in the sense that by the bijection above, every Kleisli morphism can be seen as the composition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi samp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} samp
            with a deterministic map.
          %p
            From the point of view of probability theory, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X\to X
            as taking a distribution
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in P X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            distributed according to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sampling+map"} sampling
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            This also corresponds to the idea of sampling in
            %span.newWikiWord
              probabilistic programming
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/probabilistic+programming"} ?
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monad (in computer science)
          %p Additional properties can be imposed on a representable Markov category. For example:
          %ul
            %li
              An
              %strong almost-surely-compatible representable Markov category
              is a representable Markov category in which the bijection
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:adjunction"} (2)
              preserves and reflects
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#almost_sure_equality"} almost sure equality
            %li
              An
              %strong observationally representable Markov category
              is a representable Markov category whose monad is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observational+monad"} observational
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iid"} repeated independent sampling
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
          %p
            Every observationally representable Markov category is almost surely compatible (
            %a{:href => "#supports_idemp"}> FGLPS’23, Proposition A.44
            ).
          %p
          #theorem:embed-in-representable.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p Every Markov category embeds in a representable Markov category.
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C_\mathrm{det}
              be the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathrm{det}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C_\mathrm{det}^{op}\to Set]
              and natural transformations between them. This has products, and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{y}:C_\mathrm{det}\to \hat C_\mathrm{det}
            %p
              (with
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{y}(X)=C_\mathrm{det}(-,X)
            %p
              Recall that every presheaf is a colimit of representables (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Yoneda+lemma"}> co-Yoneda lemma
              ”). We define a monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C_\mathrm{det}
              by asking that it preserves colimits and that on representables
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{y}(Y)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathbf{y}(Y))(X) = C(X,Y).
            %p
              This can be extended to a commutative, affine monad on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C_\mathrm{det}
              by using the monoidal and composition structure of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %p Moreover, by the Yoneda lemma,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X,Y) \cong \hat C_\mathrm{det}(\mathbf{y}(X)\to P(\mathbf{y}(Y)))
            %p
              and so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              full and faithfully monoidally embeds in the Kleisli category of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %p
              To see this another way, note that the identity-on-objects functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} def
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathrm{def}\to C
              amounts to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/promonad"} promonad
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} def
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathrm{def}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monad"} relative monad
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathrm{det}\to \hat C_\mathrm{det}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C_\mathrm{det}
              by left Kan extension.
            %p
              Yet another way to see this is that a Markov category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              gives rise to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+category"} Freyd category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathrm{det}\to C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathrm{det}
              is the same thing as a
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C_\mathrm{det}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} det
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C_\mathrm{det}
              is regarded as locally
              = succeed "-presentable," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
              is the trivial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sound+doctrine+of+limits"} sound doctrine of limits
              (see e.g.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#staton13"} Staton’13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+Lawvere+theory"} enriched Lawvere theory
              is the same thing as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strong monad
              that preserves the appropriate colimits, by the monad/theory correspondence.
          %p The theorem shows that we could have equivalently defined “Markov category” as “monoidal full subcategory of the Kleisli category of an affine and commutative monad on a cartesian category”.
          %p
            One important point is that probability-related properties of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            such as Kolmogorov products and positivity do not necessarily extend to the full Kleisli category of the monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on the supercategory.
          %h3#kolmogorov_products Kolmogorov products
          %p
            Kolmogorov products are a way to equip a Markov category with an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+tensor+product"} infinitary tensor product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kolmogorov+extension+theorem"} Kolmogorov extension theorem
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a Markov category, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_j)_{j\in J}
            be a family of objects. A
            %strong Kolmogorov product
            of them is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+tensor+product"} infinite tensor product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi J
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ⨂
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X_J=\bigotimes_{j\in J} X_j
          %p
            such that all finite projections (marginalizations)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi J
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_J\to X_F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\subseteq J
            finite, are deterministic morphisms.
          %p
            Using the universal property of limits, this says that a joint distribution on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_J
            is equivalently given by a family of joint distributions on all finite subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em BorelStoch
                has all countable Kolmogorov products by means of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kolmogorov+extension+theorem"} Kolmogorov extension theorem
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Set
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
          %p
            Because of this, a Kolmogorov product on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            restricts to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            in the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi det
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_det
            of deterministic morphisms.
          %h3#positivity_and_causality Positivity and causality
          %p
            Positivity and causality are two additional axioms for Markov categories, introduced in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#fritzmarkov"} Fritz’20
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
          %p
            A Markov category is called
            %strong positive
            if and only if whenever two morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
