          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Every Markov category with all conditionals is causal (
              %a{:href => "#fritzmarkov"}> Fritz’20
              , Proposition 11.34), and every causal Markov category is positive (
              %a{:href => "#dilations"}> Fritz et al’23
              , Theorem 2.24). Both converses are false.
          %p
            The category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            is causal (but it doesn’t have all conditionals).
          %h3#the_probstoch_construction
            The
            %em ProbStoch
            construction
          %p
            The
            %em ProbStoch
            construction on a Markov category formalizes the idea of a category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+spaces"} probability spaces
            %em ProbStoch
            construction gives an abstraction of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+couplings"} category of couplings
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a causal Markov category. The category
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ProbStoch
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ProbStoch(C)
            is defined as follows.
          %ul
            %li
              Its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              are pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,p)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is an object of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:I\to X
              is a state on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              Its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,p)\to(Y,q)
              are equivalence classes of morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              under
              = succeed "-almost" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ p=q
          %p The causality condition is required in order for composition to be well defined on the equivalence classes.
          %p
            In general
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ProbStoch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ProbStoch(C)
            is not a Markov category, but only a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+category"} semicartesian monoidal category
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              If a Markov category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              has all conditionals,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ProbStoch
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ProbStoch(C)
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"} dagger category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bayesian+inversion"} Bayesian inverses
          %p
            For example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BorelStoch"} BorelStoch
            has all conditionals, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ProbStoch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi BorelStoch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ProbStoch(BorelStoch)
            is the category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Krn"} Krn
          %h2#quantum_versions Quantum versions
          %p (For now, see the references.)
          %h2#History History
          %p
            In the history of Markov categories, and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+probability"} categorical probability
            in general, it happened very often that a number of independent researchers developed similar ideas unaware of each other’s work.
          %p
            The first study of a category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernels"} Markov kernels
            (here called
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere62"} Lawvere’62
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BhKaHAY8Ec8&t=3621s"} this video
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Lawvere Archives
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Lawvere+Archives"} ?
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere62b"} Lawvere’62b
          %p
            The same category was studied independently by Chentsov (
            %a{:href => "#Chentsov65"}> Chentsov’65
            ), who wrote in Russian. It is likely that Lawvere and Chentsov remained unaware of each other’s work for several decades.
          %p
            Giry (
            %a{:href => "#giry82"}> Giry’82
            ) first described
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            as the Kleisli category of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} monad which brings her name
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenio+Moggi"} Moggi
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gordon+Plotkin"} Plotkin
            started considering monads in the context of effectful programming (
            %a{:href => "#moggi89"}> Moggi’89
            ), with probabilistic computation as a special case (
            %a{:href => "#jones_plotkin89"}> Jones-Plotkin’89
            ). (More details in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monad (in computer science)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
            were introduced, see there for more.
          %p
            The first definition of a
            %em garbage-share
            (a.k.a.
            = succeed ")" do
              %em copy-discard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fabio+Gadducci"} Gadducci
            (
            %a{:href => "#gadducci96"}> Gadducci’96
            ), who in his PhD thesis introduced such a structure (for strict monoidal categories), with in mind applications to formal languages (in particular graph term rewriting). In subsequent papers together with Corradini some further properties of these construction were studied, including higher-categorical ones (
            %a{:href => "#corradini_gadducci97"}> Corradini-Gadducci’97
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#corradini_gadducci99a"} Corradini-Gadducci’99a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#corradini_gadducci99b"} Corradini-Gadducci’99b
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#corradini_gadducci02"} Corradini-Gadducci’02
          %p
            At a similar time, Carsten Führmann had worked with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/premonoidal+categories"} premonoidal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#fuhrmann99"} Fuhrmann99
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#fuhrmann02"} Fuhrmann02
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thunk-force+category"} thunk-force category
          %p
            The first mention of Markov and CD-categories used for the purpose of probability seems to have first appeared in the work of Golubtsov (
            %a{:href => "#golubtsov99"}> Golubtsov’99
            ), who named the structure “category of information converters” or “information transformers”, likely unaware of Gadducci’s work, and first writing in Russian. In subsequent work (
            %a{:href => "#golubtsov02"}> Golubtsov’02
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#golubtsov_mosaliuk02"} Golubtsov-Mosaliuk’02
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#golubtsov04"} Golubtsov’04
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+categories"} Kleisli categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#representable_markov_categories"} representable Markov category
          %p
            In parallel, and seemingly unaware of Golubtsov’s work,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"} Coecke
            and Spekkens developed a string-diagrammatic framework for Bayesian inference, for the purpose of generalizing these concept from classical to quantum (
            %a{:href => "#coecke_spekkens11"}> Coecke-Spekkens’11
            ). The resulting framework is somewhat different from the one of Markov categories, but very similar in aims and philosophy.
          %p
            Once again seemingly unaware of Golubtsov’s work,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brendan+Fong"} Fong
            studied categorical structures to model Bayesian networks in his master’s thesis (
            %a{:href => "#fong12"}> Fong’12
            ), in particular by means of equipping the objects of a category with comonoid structures.
          %p
            Around a similar time,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"} Anders Kock
            investigated the ideas of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monads"} commutative monads
            as distributions (
            %a{:href => "#kock12"}> Kock’12
            ).
          %p
            Finally, the current form of Markov and copy-discard categories, which is also the one presented in this article, was developed in the works of Cho and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"} Jacobs
            (
            %a{:href => "#cd_categories"}> Cho-Jacobs’19
            ) and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Fritz"} Fritz
            (
            %a{:href => "#fritzmarkov"}> Fritz’20
            ).
          %h2#DetailedList Detailed list
          %div
            %table
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category"} Markov category
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monad"} Probability monad
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category#conditionals"} Conditionals
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category#positivity"} Positivity
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category#kolmogorov_products"} Kolmogorov products
                %th Further references
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
                %td
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
                  on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+measurable+space"} SoberMeas
                %td
                  No (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category#fritzmarkov"}> Fritz'20
                  , Example 11.3)
                %td
                  Yes (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category#fritzmarkov"}> Fritz'20
                  , Example 11.25)
